
This directory is intended for PlatformIO Test Runner and project tests.

Unit Testing is a software testing method by which individual units of
source code, sets of one or more MCU program modules together with associated
control data, usage procedures, and operating procedures, are tested to
determine whether they are fit for use. Unit testing finds problems early
in the development cycle.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html


Memory explanation:
- The array is autofilled based on the mode, where the variable numSamples dynamically decides the amount of samples collected, saving memory.
- system utilizes 6 global float arrays of size 720, plus supporting variables:
- 6 × 720 × 4 bytes = 17,280 bytes of RAM for arrays alone
- Arduino Uno only has 2,048 bytes of RAM
- this exceeds available RAM on the board, so whilst the program may continue to compile 
and run, it risks being unstable, creating corrupted data, and unexpected crashes due to stack overflow.
- an improvement would be to dynamically reduce the size of the global arrays from 720 to 240 as it wouldnt need that many samples realistically over time, and would overflow the arduino uno's 2kb of ram, leading to whilst runnable program, it would be unstable. Alternatively, by using an Arduino MEGA with 8kb of ram it may be handle to the larger array sizes.